## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to wire config-driven per-tenant controls in a multi-DC docker-dca setup, with TLS edge and simple runtime enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for runtime limits
- Per-tenant rate limiting (token bucket)
- TLS via Traefik edge proxy
- Basic cross-DC deployment considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: token bucket per tenant with 60 rpm
const limits = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/etc/config/tenant-rate-limits.json','utf8'));
const buckets = new Map();
function allow(tenant){
  const now = Date.now();
  let b = buckets.get(tenant);
  if(!b){ b = {tokens: limits[tenant]||0, last: now}; buckets.set(tenant,b); }
  // simplistic: refill 1 token per second for 60s window
  const elapsed = (now - b.last)/1000; b.last = now; b.tokens = Math.min((limits[tenant]||0), b.tokens + elapsed);
  if(b.tokens >= 1){ b.tokens--; return true; }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you safely refresh Docker Config without restarting all containers?
- What edge cases arise with clock skew or config changes?

## Why This Is Asked
To assess practical config rotation in Swarm and application reload behavior.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config rotation with versioned configs
- Rolling updates with update-parallelism and update-delay for zero downtime
- Graceful reload semantics (SIGHUP or /reload endpoint)
- Update order start-first to ensure new containers start before old ones stop

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js reload handler
const fs = require('fs');
let flags = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/etc/flags.json','utf8'));
process.on('SIGHUP', () => {
  flags = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/etc/flags.json','utf8'));
  // apply flags to runtime features
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the zero-downtime rollout in a CI pipeline?
- What failure scenarios would prompt you to revert the config rotation?
- How would you handle config validation before rotation?
